     1	The advertising desk makes up its pages with `/app/planner/plan_edition.py`, which drops each ad on the first page with room for it, biggest earners first, and its layouts leave paying ads out of the paper and put others on pages their advertisers did not ask for. For each edition in `/app/editions/` (`mon`, `wed`, `fri` and `sat`), write a layout to `/app/layouts/<edition>.json` that follows `/app/docs/makeup-rules.md` and costs no more than the edition's target in `/app/editions/targets.json`. Each target is the cost of the best layout the desk has found for that edition.
     2	
     3	`/app/tools/check_layout.py` checks a layout under the rules and prints its cost; the rules document gives the file format. Only the four layout files are used, and how you produce them is up to you, but there is no network and nothing can be installed, so any search you run has to use what is already here (Python 3.13 and its standard library).

--- README ---
     1	# Advertising desk - ad make-up
     2	
     3	- `editions/` - the pages, sections and ads for each edition to make up, and `targets.json`
     4	- `docs/makeup-rules.md` - what a layout must do and what it costs
     5	- `tools/check_layout.py` - checks a layout under those rules and prints its cost
     6	- `planner/plan_edition.py` - the planner in use today
     7	- `layouts/` - the layouts that go to the make-up room
     8	
     9	    python3 planner/plan_edition.py editions/mon.json layouts/mon.json
    10	    python3 tools/check_layout.py editions/mon.json layouts/mon.json

--- RULES ---
     1	# Ad make-up rules
     2	
     3	These rules decide whether an edition's ad layout can go to press and what it
     4	costs. They are the rules `tools/check_layout.py` applies.
     5	
     6	## The edition
     7	
     8	Each file in `editions/` is one edition. It has `pages` pages, numbered from 1.
     9	Every page is a grid of `columns` columns (numbered from 0, left to right) and
    10	`rows` rows (numbered from 0 at the top; row `rows - 1` is the foot of the page).
    11	Odd-numbered pages are right-hand pages. Page 1 stands alone; otherwise pages
    12	`2k` and `2k + 1` face each other as one spread (so 2 faces 3, 4 faces 5, and
    13	an even last page faces nothing).
    14	
    15	`sections` gives, for each section name, its first and last page (inclusive).
    16	
    17	An ad has an `id`, a `width` in columns and a `depth` in rows, a `rate` (what
    18	the paper earns when it runs), `booked` (true when it has to run in this
    19	edition), a `section` it asked for (or `null`), `right_hand` (true when it asked
    20	for a right-hand page) and a `competitor_group` (or `null`). `advertiser` is for
    21	the desk's information and plays no part in the rules.
    22	
    23	The edition also gives `front_page_ad_rows`, `max_ad_share_percent`,
    24	`wrong_section_percent` and `left_hand_percent`.
    25	
    26	## What a layout must do
    27	
    28	A layout places some of the ads. Each placed ad sits on one page, with its top
    29	left cell at a `column` and `row`, and covers `width` columns and `depth` rows
    30	from there. Then:
    31	
    32	1. Every placed ad lies inside its page, and no two ads on a page share a cell.
    33	2. Every placed ad rests on the foot of the page or on ads: either its bottom row
    34	   is the page's last row, or every cell directly below its bottom row, across
    35	   its whole width, is covered by some ad.
    36	3. On page 1, ads cover only the bottom `front_page_ad_rows` rows, so at most
    37	   `front_page_ad_rows * columns` cells. On every other page, ads cover at most
    38	   `max_ad_share_percent * rows * columns // 100` cells (whole-number division).
    39	4. Two ads of the same `competitor_group` are never on the same page or on the
    40	   two pages of one spread.
    41	5. Every `booked` ad is placed. Other ads may be left out.
    42	
    43	## Cost
    44	
    45	A layout costs the sum of:
    46	
    47	- the `rate` of every ad left out;
    48	- for every placed ad that asked for a section and is on a page outside it,
    49	  `rate * wrong_section_percent // 100`;
    50	- for every placed ad that asked for a right-hand page and is on an even page,
    51	  `rate * left_hand_percent // 100`.
    52	
    53	(`//` is whole-number division, rounding down, taken separately for each ad.)
    54	Both of the last two can apply to the same ad. Costs are whole numbers, and
    55	lower is better.
    56	
    57	## File format
    58	
    59	    {"placements": {"AD001": {"page": 3, "column": 0, "row": 21}, ...}}
    60	
    61	Each key is the id of a placed ad, given once; `page`, `column` and `row` are
    62	whole numbers (JSON integers, not strings or booleans). Ads not listed are left
    63	out. Unknown ad ids are an error. Any other top-level key is ignored.

